<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanah Datar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanah Datar is a regency in the highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, with its seat at Batusangkar. It is one of the three original heartlands (luhak) of the Minangkabau and contains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagaruyung">Pagaruyung</a>, the seat of the Minangkabau kings, and many traditional villages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanah-datar.jpg" alt="Tanah Datar" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rodney Ee · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sesudut_Sawah_di_Tanah_Datar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means Flat Land. The landlocked regency lies in the middle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> province, between Mount Marapi and Mount Singgalang, at an average altitude of 400 to 1,000 metres. Its land is mostly hilly and takes in two thirds of Lake Singkarak. The climate is moderate, with temperatures between 12 °C and 25 °C and more than 3,000 millimetres of rain a year, most of it falling from September to February, which supports extensive farming.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many relics of Minangkabau tradition survive in the regency, both as objects and as customary institutions. Among its historic sites are the Pagaruyung Palace, or Istano Basa Pagaruyuang, with its museum, the Adityawarman Inscription and other stone inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Malay from the 14th century, several sites with standing megaliths known as batu tagak, the Batu Basurek and Batu Batikam stones, and Fort van der Capellen.</p><p>The old village of Pariangan, the Balimbing tower house, the Lubuk Bauk surau and the village of Pandai Sikek, where the traditional songket cloth called kain balapak is woven, are also among its historic places, as are the Balai Ruang Sari and the Istano Rajo. The pledge Adat Basandi Syarak, Syarak Basandi Kitabullah, also known as the Satie Oath, came from Bukit Marapalam at Puncak Pato, in the North Lintau Buo district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tanah Datar covers 1,337.1 square kilometres and is divided into fourteen districts; its seat is the town of Batusangkar. The city of Padang Panjang lies within it but is a municipality of its own. The regency had 371,704 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a>.</p><p>It is an agricultural area: more than 70% of its people work in food crops, plantations, fisheries and animal husbandry, and tourism and small industry also rest on farming. The traditional bull race pacu jawi is held in the regency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanah Datar Regency” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1347 Adityawarman, a prince who had served Majapahit, proclaimed himself king of Malayu at Dharmasraya and moved his court up into the Minangkabau highlands, near Pagaruyung in the <b>Tanah Datar</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/pagaruyung-kingdom">Pagaruyung Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
